  "sprintRules": [
    "The 88-Day Level II Plan",
    "Rule 1: Level II is item sets, not standalone questions. Every practice question you do should sit under a vignette. Reading the vignette fast and pulling the three numbers that matter is a separate skill from knowing the material.",
    "Rule 2: The EOCQ method still holds. Watch the EOCQ video. Pause when the question appears. Solve it. Unpause. Write down the logic, not the answer.",
    "Rule 3: You have 88 days, not 24. That means you can watch the main videos this time - but only once, and only at speed. If a module is a re-run of Level I, go straight to the EOCQ.",
    "Rule 4: Heavyweights first. Equity Valuation, Fixed Income, FSA and Portfolio Management are each 10-15%. Together they are over half the exam.",
    "Rule 5: Sunday is consolidation, not new material. Rework the week's misses. If you skip this, the week does not stick.",
    "Rule 6: Zero-excuse discipline. From 6:15 PM to 10:00 PM on weeknights, and your weekend outside of chores, belongs to this plan."
  ],
  "examDate": "2026-11-21T08:30:00",
  "reviewGuideRaw": "# CFA Level II - Review & Traps\n\n**Exam: Saturday, November 21, 2026**\n\nThis guide fills in as each topic gets built out. Fixed Income is first, off the Mark Meldrum Level II slides. Your full Level I guide is still here - use the Level I toggle above.\n\n## **What actually changes from Level I**\n\n**The format is the exam.** Level II is item sets: a vignette of roughly one page, then four or six questions on it. You are not being asked whether you know a definition. You are being asked to find the three relevant numbers in a page of noise and run one calculation with them.\n\n- Read the questions before the vignette. It tells you what to look for.\n- Exhibits carry most of the data. Most vignettes include at least one number that exists only to be ignored.\n- Roughly 18 minutes per item set. If a question stalls you, flag it and move.\n\n**The material is deeper, not wider.** Ten topics, 45 learning modules, against 103 at Level I. Each module carries far more.\n\n**Weights are flatter.** Nothing dominates the way Ethics did at Level I:\n\n| Topic | Weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| Financial Statement Analysis | 10-15% |\n| Equities | 10-15% |\n| Fixed Income | 10-15% |\n| Portfolio Construction | 10-15% |\n| Ethical and Professional Standards | 10-15% |\n| Quantitative Methods | 5-10% |\n| Economics | 5-10% |\n| Corporate Finance | 5-10% |\n| Derivatives and Risk Management | 5-10% |\n| Alternative Investments | 5-10% |\n\nSource: CFA Institute, Level II exam page.\n\n## **Ethics at Level II**\n\nSame Code and Standards you already know. What changes is that the vignettes are long and the violation is usually buried in a detail two paragraphs from the question. Read for the facts, then apply the Standard. When duties conflict, the client comes first.\n\n## **Fixed Income - coming next**\n\nTerm structure, arbitrage-free valuation, bonds with embedded options, credit analysis models, credit default swaps. Formulas, worked traps and drills land here first.\n",
